"""Original teaching, using only the mathematical sequence of book sections 9.1–9.5."""
lessons=[]
models=[]
def ex(code,title,question,solution,model):
    models.append(dict(id=code,**model))
    return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=code)
def lesson(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
    lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='stats_inference_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method=method,mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-statistics/topics/stats_inference_'
lesson('one_sample','Student’s t and a single population mean',
 'Estimate an unknown variance, select the tail and distinguish evidence from proof.',
 'Carry out and interpret one-sample t-tests with the correct degrees of freedom.',
 '<p>Review sample means, variance, the normal distribution, hypotheses and significance levels.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Estimating the variability</h3><p>For n independent observations, the unbiased estimate of population variance is</p>
 \[s^2=\frac{\sum(x-\bar x)^2}{n-1}=\frac{\sum x^2-(\sum x)^2/n}{n-1}.\]
 <p>Here s is a sample standard deviation. The estimated standard error of the mean is s/√n; it describes variation between sample means, rather than between individual observations. Subtracting a coding constant changes the mean but leaves the variance unchanged.</p>
 <h3>Why a t distribution?</h3><p>For a random sample from a normal population with unknown variance,</p>
 \[T=\frac{\bar X-\mu_0}{S/\sqrt n}\sim t_{n-1}\quad\text{under }H_0:\mu=\mu_0.\]
 <p>The extra uncertainty from estimating variance produces heavier tails than the standard normal distribution. The t distribution is symmetric about zero and approaches the standard normal as the degrees of freedom grow. A small sample size alone does not establish normality.</p>
 <h3>Choose the alternative before looking at the result</h3><p>For H₁: μ&gt;μ₀ use the upper tail; for μ&lt;μ₀ use the lower tail. For μ≠μ₀ split the significance level equally between both tails. Write t<sub>ν,p</sub> for the p-quantile, so P(T≤t<sub>ν,p</sub>)=p. A two-sided p-value is twice the smaller tail probability.</p>
 <p>Use the convention stated in the question. In this chapter reject when p≤α, including the critical boundary. Otherwise do not reject: this is insufficient evidence against the null, not proof that it is true. Express the conclusion about the population mean in the original units.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Identify the population parameter and check random independent sampling and normality.</li><li>State H₀ and a directional or two-sided H₁.</li><li>Calculate s² and the standard error without early rounding.</li><li>Calculate t and use n−1 degrees of freedom.</li><li>Compare the correct tail with α, then conclude in context.</li></ol>',
 ['Use n−1 in the unbiased variance, but √n in the standard error.','Do not use a two-sided critical value for a one-sided claim.','A negative statistic is not evidence in an upper-tail test.','Not rejecting a null does not prove it.'],
 [ex('INF-E-S1','1 · Separate variance from standard error',r'<p>For a normal population, a random independent sample has n=9, Σx=108 and Σx²=1320. Find the mean, unbiased variance and estimated standard error.</p>',r'\[\bar x=12,\quad s^2=\frac{1320-108^2/9}{8}=3,\quad\mathrm{SE}=\frac{\sqrt3}{3}.\]<p>Variance has squared measurement units. The standard error has the same units as the mean.</p>',dict(kind='summary',n=9,total='108',squares='1320',expected=['12','3','sqrt(3)/3'])),
 ex('INF-E-S2','2 · An upper-tail test',r'<p>Nine independently sampled normal processing times have mean 41 minutes and unbiased variance 9. Test H₀: μ=39 against H₁: μ&gt;39 at 5%, using the supplied critical value 1.860.</p>',r'\[t=\frac{41-39}{3/\sqrt9}=2,\qquad\nu=8.\]<p>Since 2&gt;1.860, reject H₀. There is sufficient evidence at 5% that the population mean processing time exceeds 39 minutes. The threshold is for an upper tail, not a central 95% interval.</p>',dict(kind='test',n=9,mean='41',variance='9',null='39',critical='1.860',alternative='greater',expected=['2','8'],reject=True)),
 ex('INF-E-S3','3 · Both tails matter',r'<p>A normal population with unknown variance gives n=16, mean 17.5 and s²=4. Test μ=18 against μ≠18 at 5%, using the supplied positive critical value 2.131.</p>',r'\[t=\frac{17.5-18}{2/4}=-1,\qquad\nu=15.\]<p>The rejection region is t≤−2.131 or t≥2.131. The statistic lies between these limits, so do not reject. The data do not provide sufficient evidence of a difference from 18; they do not establish equality.</p>',dict(kind='test',n=16,mean='35/2',variance='4',null='18',critical='2.131',alternative='two',expected=['-1','15'],reject=False)),
 ex('INF-E-S4','4 · Undo coding without changing the variance',r'<p>A sample of ten normal observations is coded by u=x−50. The summaries are Σu=8 and Σu²=20.8. Find the mean of x, its unbiased variance and the standard error.</p>',r'\[\bar x=50+\frac8{10}=50.8,\quad s^2=\frac{20.8-8^2/10}{9}=\frac85,\quad\mathrm{SE}=\frac25.\]<p>The added 50 belongs in the mean. It must not be added to the variance.</p>',dict(kind='summary',n=10,total='8',squares='20.8',offset='50',expected=['50.8','8/5','2/5']))])
lesson('normal','Independent means: the normal procedure',
 'Distinguish known population variances from large-sample estimated variances.',
 'Test a specified difference of independent population means with an appropriate normal model.',
 '<p>Review <a href="'+base+'one_sample">hypotheses and standard errors</a>, independent random variables and the central limit theorem.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Independence makes variances add</h3>
 \[\operatorname{Var}(\bar X-\bar Y)=\frac{\sigma_X^2}{n_X}+\frac{\sigma_Y^2}{n_Y}.\]
 <p>Even though the sample means are subtracted, their variances add. This formula requires independent samples; it is not the formula for matched pairs.</p>
 <h3>Two routes to a normal statistic</h3><p>If both populations are normal and their variances are known, use the known variances: the standardised difference is exactly normal at any sample sizes. If the variances are unknown but both samples are sufficiently large for the central limit approximation, estimate them separately by unbiased sample variances:</p>
 \[\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}=\sqrt{\frac{s_X^2}{n_X}+\frac{s_Y^2}{n_Y}},\qquad z=\frac{(\bar x-\bar y)-\delta_0}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}}.\]
 <p>The latter procedure is approximate, needs independent random samples with finite population variances and does not require equal variances. Sample size does not cure a biased or dependent sampling scheme. For small independent normal samples with a common unknown variance, use the <a href="'''+base+r'''pooled">pooled t procedure</a> instead.</p>
 <h3>A non-zero null difference</h3><p>To investigate whether A exceeds B by more than 2 units, use H₀: μA−μB=2 and H₁: μA−μB&gt;2, not a null of zero. Retain the same order of subtraction throughout. Reversing a contrast reverses its statistic and alternative together.</p>
 <h3>Significance as a range</h3><p>If α is expressed in percent and the calculated p-value is p, rejection occurs for 100p≤α&lt;100 under the stated boundary convention. Non-rejection occurs for 0&lt;α&lt;100p. Compare probabilities with probabilities, not with percentages.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Define the order of the population mean difference.</li><li>Check whether variances are known or are being estimated with a large-sample approximation.</li><li>Add the variances of the two sample means.</li><li>Subtract the hypothesised difference and standardise.</li><li>Use the appropriate normal tail and report the evidence in context.</li></ol>',
 ['Subtract means, but add their variance contributions.','Do not pool unequal unknown variances merely because samples are large.','Subtract the non-zero null difference when one is stated.','A significance level of 5% is the probability 0.05.'],
 [ex('INF-E-N1','1 · Known variances and an exact normal model',r'<p>Independent normal populations have known variances 9 and 16. Samples of sizes 9 and 16 have means 14 and 12. Find the standard error and the z statistic for equal population means.</p>',r'\[\mathrm{SE}=\sqrt{9/9+16/16}=\sqrt2,\quad z=\frac2{\sqrt2}=\sqrt2.\]<p>The normal model is exact because the populations are normal and the variances are known. No t degrees of freedom are needed.</p>',dict(kind='contrast',nx=9,ny=16,mx='14',my='12',vx='9',vy='16',null='0',expected=['sqrt(2)','sqrt(2)'])),
 ex('INF-E-N2','2 · Unknown variances, large samples',r'<p>Independent random samples have nA=64, nB=100, means 23 and 22, and unbiased variance estimates 16 and 25. Use a large-sample normal approximation to test equal means against different means at 5%, using 1.960.</p>',r'\[\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}=\sqrt{16/64+25/100}=\frac1{\sqrt2},\quad z=\sqrt2.\]<p>Since |z|&lt;1.960, do not reject. There is insufficient evidence of different population means. Neither equal population variances nor normal individual observations were assumed; the approximation uses the independent large samples.</p>',dict(kind='contrast',nx=64,ny=100,mx='23',my='22',vx='16',vy='25',null='0',expected=['sqrt(2)/2','sqrt(2)'])),
 ex('INF-E-N3','3 · A minimum useful difference',r'<p>Independent large samples give means 8.4 and 7.2 and an estimated standard error of 0.4. Test whether μA−μB exceeds 0.5 at 5%, using the supplied upper-tail critical value 1.645.</p>',r'\[H_0:\mu_A-\mu_B=0.5,\quad H_1:\mu_A-\mu_B>0.5,\quad z=\frac{1.2-0.5}{0.4}=1.75.\]<p>Reject H₀ since 1.75&gt;1.645. The evidence concerns a gain above 0.5, not simply a gain above zero.</p>',dict(kind='given_se',estimate='1.2',se='0.4',null='0.5',expected=['7/4'])),
 ex('INF-E-N4','4 · A p-value determines the whole range',r'<p>A one-sided normal test has calculated p-value 0.032. With α measured in percent, for which levels is H₀ rejected? The rule is p≤α/100.</p>',r'<p>The range is \(3.2\le\alpha&lt;100\). At 1% do not reject; at 5% reject. The boundary 3.2% is included because equality is in the rejection rule.</p>',dict(kind='percent',p='0.032',expected=['16/5']))])
lesson('pooled','Pooled variance and independent-sample t-tests',
 'Weight two variance estimates by their degrees of freedom before comparing means.',
 'Calculate a common variance estimate and use a two-sample t statistic with the correct assumptions.',
 '<p>Review unbiased variance and <a href="'+base+'normal">independent means</a>. Know the difference between independent and paired observations.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Pooling has an assumption</h3><p>For independent random samples from two normal populations with the same unknown population variance, combine the centred sums of squares:</p>
 \[s_p^2=\frac{(n_X-1)s_X^2+(n_Y-1)s_Y^2}{n_X+n_Y-2}.\]
 <p>This is a degrees-of-freedom weighted estimate of a common variance. It is not generally the arithmetic average of two sample variances. Normality and a common population variance must be given or justified; similar observed variances alone do not establish either condition.</p>
 <h3>Standardise the difference</h3>
 \[t=\frac{(\bar x-\bar y)-\delta_0}{s_p\sqrt{1/n_X+1/n_Y}},\qquad\nu=n_X+n_Y-2.\]
 <p>Under the null this statistic has a t distribution with ν degrees of freedom. The denominator uses the square root of the pooled variance. Choose the one- or two-sided critical value from the alternative, not from whether the observed difference happens to be positive.</p>
 <h3>Reconstructing missing information</h3><p>A stated pooled variance may give an equation for an unknown sample size. After solving, check sample sizes are integers, exceed 1 and produce non-negative centred sums of squares. An algebraic root outside that domain is not a possible sample size. To estimate the common standard deviation, take the square root only after pooling the variances.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Check independent samples, normal populations and a common unknown variance.</li><li>Find each mean and centred sum of squares.</li><li>Pool the centred sums using the combined degrees of freedom.</li><li>Calculate the standard error and t statistic for the stated null.</li><li>Use nX+nY−2 degrees of freedom and conclude in context.</li></ol>',
 ['Pooling standard deviations is not pooling variances.','Weight by n−1, not n.','Use the two-sample degrees of freedom, not the smaller sample size minus one.','Equal variances do not make paired observations independent.'],
 [ex('INF-E-P1','1 · Unequal sizes require unequal weights',r'<p>Independent samples of sizes 6 and 10 have unbiased variances 4 and 8. Their populations share a variance. Estimate the common variance and standard deviation.</p>',r'\[s_p^2=\frac{5(4)+9(8)}{14}=\frac{46}{7},\qquad\widehat\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{46}{7}}.\]<p>The unweighted average 6 would be incorrect.</p>',dict(kind='pool',nx=6,ny=10,vx='4',vy='8',expected=['46/7','sqrt(46/7)'])),
 ex('INF-E-P2','2 · From pooling to a conclusion',r'<p>Independent normal samples have sizes 5 and 5, means 18 and 16, and unbiased variances 2 and 3. Assume a common unknown variance. Test equality against a positive A−B difference at 5%, using 1.860.</p>',r'\[s_p^2=\frac{4(2)+4(3)}8=\frac52,\quad\mathrm{SE}=\sqrt{\frac52\left(\frac15+\frac15\right)}=1,\quad t=2.\]<p>There are 8 degrees of freedom. Since 2&gt;1.860, reject H₀: there is sufficient evidence of a greater population mean for A.</p>',dict(kind='pool_test',nx=5,ny=5,vx='2',vy='3',mx='18',my='16',null='0',expected=['5/2','1','2','8'])),
 ex('INF-E-P3','3 · A specified non-zero difference',r'<p>Independent normal samples from equal-variance populations have sizes 8 and 8, means 12.5 and 11, and unbiased variances 4 and 4. Find the statistic for H₀: μA−μB=0.5.</p>',r'\[s_p^2=4,\quad\mathrm{SE}=\sqrt{4(1/8+1/8)}=1,\quad t=\frac{1.5-0.5}{1}=1,\quad\nu=14.\]<p>The appropriate critical tail would depend on the stated alternative.</p>',dict(kind='pool_test',nx=8,ny=8,vx='4',vy='4',mx='12.5',my='11',null='0.5',expected=['4','1','1','14'])),
 ex('INF-E-P4','4 · Recover a missing variance',r'<p>Samples of sizes 7 and 11 have pooled variance 5. The first unbiased sample variance is 3. Find the second unbiased variance.</p>',r'\[16(5)=6(3)+10s_Y^2,\qquad s_Y^2=\frac{31}{5}.\]<p>The estimate is positive. Both the numerator weights and the denominator come from degrees of freedom.</p>',dict(kind='missing_variance',nx=7,ny=11,vx='3',pooled='5',expected=['31/5']))])
lesson('paired','Paired observations and mean differences',
 'Keep the matching, define the direction and analyse one sample of differences.',
 'Carry out paired t-tests and identify which normality and independence assumptions are required.',
 '<p>Review <a href="'+base+'one_sample">one-sample t-tests</a>. Put both readings in the same units before subtraction.</p>',
 r'''<h3>The pair is the unit of analysis</h3><p>Before-and-after measurements on the same object are dependent. Matched pairs may also be formed before random allocation. Define d consistently, for example before minus after when positive means a reduction. There are n differences from n pairs, even though there are 2n readings.</p>
 \[\bar d=\frac{\sum d}{n},\qquad s_d^2=\frac{\sum d^2-(\sum d)^2/n}{n-1},\qquad t=\frac{\bar d-\delta_0}{s_d/\sqrt n}.\]
 <p>Use n−1 degrees of freedom. The normality assumption concerns the population of differences, not necessarily each original population. Different pairs must be independent, while the two readings within each pair need not be.</p>
 <h3>Do not discard the cross-product</h3>
 \[\sum d=\sum x-\sum y,\qquad\sum d^2=\sum x^2+\sum y^2-2\sum xy\quad(d=x-y).\]
 <p>Subtracting the two sample variances ignores the dependence. If only summary data are given, the cross-product supplies the missing information. Adding the same correction to both readings in a pair leaves its difference unchanged.</p>
 <h3>Interpret direction and assumptions</h3><p>If d=before−after, a claim of a reduction greater than c uses H₁: μd&gt;c. If you reverse d, also reverse the null value and alternative. A strong outlier or skew in a small sample makes the normal-difference model questionable. A sign test for a population median is a different procedure and parameter; it is not a t-test for the mean.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Identify the matching and convert to common units.</li><li>Define the signed within-pair difference.</li><li>Find its mean and unbiased variance, retaining all pairs.</li><li>Apply a one-sample t-test to those differences.</li><li>State assumptions about the differences and interpret the sign in context.</li></ol>',
 ['Use n pairs, not 2n separate observations.','Normality is required for the population of differences.','Do not pool the original sample variances for paired data.','Changing minutes to seconds must precede subtraction.'],
 [ex('INF-E-D1','1 · Work with five differences',r'<p>Five independent matched pairs give before-minus-after differences 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Find their mean, unbiased variance and standard error.</p>',r'\[\bar d=3,\quad s_d^2=\frac{55-15^2/5}{4}=\frac52,\quad\mathrm{SE}=\frac1{\sqrt2}.\]<p>There are five observations in the difference sample, not ten.</p>',dict(kind='raw',raw=[1,2,3,4,5],expected=['3','5/2','sqrt(2)/2'])),
 ex('INF-E-D2','2 · Use cross-products',r'<p>For six independent pairs, Σx=120, Σy=108, Σx²=2420, Σy²=1964 and Σxy=2174. Find the mean and unbiased variance of d=x−y.</p>',r'\[\sum d=12,\quad\sum d^2=2420+1964-2(2174)=36,\quad\bar d=2,\quad s_d^2=\frac{36-12^2/6}{5}=\frac{12}{5}.\]<p>The cross-product must be included.</p>',dict(kind='cross',n=6,sx='120',sy='108',xx='2420',yy='1964',xy='2174',expected=['2','12/5'])),
 ex('INF-E-D3','3 · Test an improvement threshold',r'<p>For nine independent pairs, the normal population of before-minus-after differences has unknown variance. The sample gives mean 1.4 and s²=0.81. Test whether the population mean reduction exceeds 0.8 at 5%, using 1.860.</p>',r'\[H_0:\mu_d=0.8,\quad H_1:\mu_d>0.8,\quad t=\frac{1.4-0.8}{0.9/3}=2,\quad\nu=8.\]<p>Reject H₀. There is sufficient evidence of a population mean reduction greater than 0.8 units, assuming normal differences and independent pairs.</p>',dict(kind='test',n=9,mean='1.4',variance='0.81',null='0.8',critical='1.860',alternative='greater',expected=['2','8'],reject=True)),
 ex('INF-E-D4','4 · Convert units before pairing',r'<p>A task takes 5 minutes before a change and 270 seconds afterwards. Find the reduction in minutes. What happens if both readings for this pair were recorded 6 seconds too high?</p>',r'<p>The after time is 270/60=4.5 minutes, so the reduction is 0.5 minutes. Correcting both readings subtracts 0.1 minute from each. Their difference remains 0.5, so the paired statistic is unchanged.</p>',dict(kind='units',before='5',after_seconds='270',correction_seconds='6',expected=['1/2']))])
lesson('intervals','Confidence intervals for one mean',
 'Construct, interpret and reverse an interval without confusing a mean with individual readings.',
 'Find t intervals for a population mean and reconstruct sample summaries from a supplied interval.',
 '<p>Review <a href="'+base+'one_sample">unbiased variance and t quantiles</a>. For paired means apply the same method to differences.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Estimate plus or minus uncertainty</h3><p>For independent random observations from a normal population with unknown variance, a central 100(1−α)% interval is</p>
 \[\bar x\pm t_{n-1,1-\alpha/2}\frac{s}{\sqrt n}.\]
 <p>Use a two-sided critical value and give both endpoints, units and confidence level. Keep full precision until the requested final rounding. If the population variance is known, a normal-population mean instead uses σ/√n with a normal critical value.</p>
 <h3>What confidence means</h3><p>The population mean is fixed. Under repeated independent random sampling, the stated percentage of intervals produced by this procedure would contain it. The interval is not a range containing that percentage of individual observations. Dependence, selection bias or an incorrect normal model can invalidate its advertised coverage.</p>
 <h3>Reverse the construction</h3><p>For endpoints L and U, let m=(L+U)/2 and h=(U−L)/2. When the supplied critical value c is to be treated as exact,</p>
 \[\bar x=m,\quad s^2=n\left(\frac h c\right)^2,\quad\sum x=n\bar x,\quad\sum x^2=(n-1)s^2+n\bar x^2.\]
 <p>Use the critical value stated in the problem for a reverse calculation; replacing a tabulated value by a more precise quantile changes the problem's data. An interval based on rounded endpoints may otherwise not determine exact summaries.</p>
 <h3>Connect tests and intervals carefully</h3><p>A central 100(1−α)% interval corresponds to a two-sided α test under the same model. Interior values are not rejected. At an endpoint, follow the stated equality convention: when p≤α is rejected, that boundary value is rejected. A central 95% interval does not directly supply a one-sided 5% test.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Identify the parameter, model and standard error.</li><li>Find the two-sided t quantile with n−1 degrees of freedom.</li><li>Calculate the margin and both endpoints, rounding only at the end.</li><li>For reverse questions use the midpoint and half-width first.</li><li>Interpret repeated-sampling coverage and check any requested test has the matching tails.</li></ol>',
 ['The confidence level concerns the procedure, not the proportion of individual observations.','A 95% interval uses 2.5% in each tail.','Do not replace a supplied exact tabulated value in a reverse problem.','Give both endpoints in increasing order.'],
 [ex('INF-E-I1','1 · An interval using a supplied critical value',r'<p>For a normal population with unknown variance, n=9, sample mean 30 and s²=9. Construct the interval using the supplied central critical value 2.306, treating it as exact.</p>',r'\[\mathrm{SE}=1,\quad 30\pm2.306=(27.694,32.306).\]<p>For a central 95% t interval there are 8 degrees of freedom. Both endpoints are required; the mean alone is not the interval.</p>',dict(kind='interval',n=9,mean='30',variance='9',critical='2.306',expected=['27.694','32.306'])),
 ex('INF-E-I2','2 · Recover the variance',r'<p>A t interval for a normal mean from n=16 has endpoints 20 and 24. It used the supplied critical value 2 exactly. Recover the sample mean, unbiased variance, Σx and Σx².</p>',r'\[\bar x=22,\quad h=2,\quad s^2=16(2/2)^2=16,\quad\sum x=352,\quad\sum x^2=15(16)+16(22)^2=7984.\]<p>The stipulated critical value is data for this reconstruction, not a value to recalculate.</p>',dict(kind='reverse',n=16,lower='20',upper='24',critical='2',expected=['22','16','352','7984'])),
 ex('INF-E-I3','3 · Reverse an interval for a paired mean',r'<p>An interval for a population mean reduction from nine independent normal differences is (0.4,1.6), calculated with the stipulated critical value 2. Recover the mean and unbiased variance of the differences.</p>',r'\[\bar d=1,\quad h=0.6,\quad s_d^2=9(0.6/2)^2=\frac{81}{100}.\]<p>These summaries describe the differences, not either original sample separately.</p>',dict(kind='reverse',n=9,lower='0.4',upper='1.6',critical='2',expected=['1','81/100','9','387/25'])),
 ex('INF-E-I4','4 · Solve a rejection boundary',r'<p>A normal sample gives n=9, mean 14 and s²=9. An upper-tail t test uses critical value c=2. Find all null values k rejected by H₁: μ&gt;k, including the critical boundary.</p>',r'\[\frac{14-k}{1}\ge2\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad k\le12.\]<p>Smaller proposed means are contradicted in the upper direction. Under the stated rule k=12 is rejected as well.</p>',dict(kind='threshold',n=9,mean='14',variance='9',critical='2',alternative='greater',expected=['12']))])
lesson('differences','Confidence intervals for differences of means',
 'Select a normal, pooled t or paired interval and preserve the order of subtraction.',
 'Construct complete intervals for population mean contrasts and use them to interpret compatible tests.',
 '<p>Review <a href="'+base+'normal">normal comparisons</a>, <a href="'+base+'pooled">pooled variance</a> and <a href="'+base+'paired">paired data</a>.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Start with the design, not a memorised critical value</h3><p>For independent populations with known variances and normal sample means, use</p>
 \[(\bar x-\bar y)\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{\sigma_X^2/n_X+\sigma_Y^2/n_Y}.\]
 <p>This is exact for normal populations with known variances. For independent large samples with unknown finite variances, substitute separate unbiased variance estimates; the interval is then a normal approximation and needs no equal-variance assumption.</p>
 <h3>Small normal samples with a common unknown variance</h3>
 \[(\bar x-\bar y)\pm t_{n_X+n_Y-2,1-\alpha/2}\,s_p\sqrt{1/n_X+1/n_Y}.\]
 <p>Pool only when a common population variance is assumed. For matched pairs use the one-mean t interval for the differences, with n−1 degrees of freedom. Pairing and pooling are different designs.</p>
 <h3>Reverse an interval correctly</h3><p>If (L,U) estimates μA−μB, then (−U,−L) estimates μB−μA at the same confidence level. Negating without reversing the endpoints produces an incorrectly ordered interval.</p>
 <h3>Interpret the whole interval</h3><p>An interval wholly above zero supports a positive difference in population means, not a claim that every A observation exceeds every B observation. An interval with zero strictly inside is compatible with equality for the matching two-sided test; it does not prove equality. An interval wholly below zero supports the opposite direction. Use the same model, confidence level and boundary convention for a test comparison. A one-sided improvement threshold requires its own tail calculation.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Define the contrast and identify independent or paired data.</li><li>Select known-variance normal, large-sample normal, pooled t or paired t.</li><li>Find its standard error and central critical value.</li><li>Give both endpoints in the stated order and units.</li><li>Interpret the parameter difference and any matching two-sided test.</li></ol>',
 ['Small samples do not require t when normal-population variances are known.','Use (−U,−L) when reversing the contrast.','Zero inside an interval does not prove equal means.','The interval concerns means, not every individual comparison.'],
 [ex('INF-E-C1','1 · A large-sample interval',r'<p>Independent large samples have sizes 100 and 100, means 51 and 50, and unbiased variances 9 and 16. Construct an approximate 95% interval for μA−μB using 1.960 exactly.</p>',r'\[\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}=\sqrt{9/100+16/100}=\frac12,\quad 1\pm1.960(1/2)=(0.020,1.980).\]<p>The interval is positive. The corresponding two-sided 5% normal-approximation test rejects equality; no equal-variance assumption was needed.</p>',dict(kind='contrast_interval',nx=100,ny=100,mx='51',my='50',vx='9',vy='16',critical='1.960',pooled=False,expected=['1/50','99/50'])),
 ex('INF-E-C2','2 · A pooled t interval',r'<p>Independent normal samples have sizes 5 and 5, means 10 and 9, and unbiased variances 2 and 3. Assume a common variance. Use the supplied central critical value 2.306 to construct the interval.</p>',r'\[s_p^2=5/2,\quad\mathrm{SE}=1,\quad\nu=8,\quad 1\pm2.306=(-1.306,3.306).\]<p>Zero is inside. Do not reject equality in the corresponding two-sided test, but do not claim the means are equal.</p>',dict(kind='contrast_interval',nx=5,ny=5,mx='10',my='9',vx='2',vy='3',critical='2.306',pooled=True,expected=['-1.306','3.306'])),
 ex('INF-E-C3','3 · Small samples with known variances',r'<p>Two independent normal populations have known variances 4 and 9. Samples of sizes 16 and 36 have means 8 and 7. Use the supplied normal critical value 1.645 for a central 90% interval.</p>',r'\[\mathrm{SE}=\sqrt{4/16+9/36}=\frac1{\sqrt2},\quad\left(1-\frac{1.645}{\sqrt2},\ 1+\frac{1.645}{\sqrt2}\right).\]<p>The known population variances make the normal procedure appropriate despite small samples. The stipulated rounded critical value is used as supplied.</p>',dict(kind='contrast_interval',nx=16,ny=36,mx='8',my='7',vx='4',vy='9',critical='1.645',pooled=False,expected=['1-329*sqrt(2)/400','1+329*sqrt(2)/400'])),
 ex('INF-E-C4','4 · Change the order of the contrast',r'<p>A central interval for μA−μB is (−3.4,−0.8). Give the corresponding interval for μB−μA and interpret its sign.</p>',r'<p>The reversed interval is (0.8,3.4). Both endpoints are positive, supporting a greater population mean for B under the model. This says nothing about every possible pair of individual observations.</p>',dict(kind='reverse_contrast',lower='-3.4',upper='-0.8',expected=['4/5','17/5']))])
